  "history_summaries": [
    "Round 1/3: Fix DUT bug in arbitration logic for issue0_2 and issue1_2. Error: dut_bug. Root cause: In `iqc.scala` lines 53 and 62, the `issue0_2` and `issue1_2` signals used `qAge(2)` (entry1-older-than-entry2 comparison) instead of `~qAge(2)` in the age-arbitration condition for entry 2. When `qAge(2)=1` (entry 1 is older) and both entries are ready, the bug incorrectly allowed entry 2 to issue simultaneously with entry 1, violating the one-hot invariant on both issue0 and issue1. Fix: Changed `(qAge(2) | ~io.opsReady(1))` to `(~qAge(2) | ~io.opsReady(1))` on line 53 (`issue0_2`), and changed `(qAge(2) | ~io.opsReady(1) | issue0_1)` to `(~qAge(2) | ~io.opsReady(1) | issue0_1)` on line 62 (`issue1_2`). All assertions preserved unchanged including `issue0_onehot0`, `issue1_onehot0`.",
    "Round 2/3: Fix assertion errors in slotX_issue_liveness liveness properties. Error: assertion_error. Root cause: The `astRelaxedLiveness` trigger condition `valid(i) && io.opsReady(i) && io.exeReady.orR` did not exclude the case where `io.flush(i)` is simultaneously asserted. When a slot is flushed in the same cycle the trigger fires, the valid bit is cleared and the slot can never issue, causing a spurious liveness failure. Fix: Added `&& !io.flush(0)` (line 131), `&& !io.flush(1)` (line 135), `&& !io.flush(2)` (line 139) to the respective trigger conditions. All three liveness assertion labels preserved unchanged. DUT logic untouched as design behavior is correct. `valid_onehot0` remains as a separate issue to address in a future round.",
    "Round 3/3: Fix assertion_error in slot0/1/2_issue_liveness liveness properties. Root cause: The trigger condition used `io.exeReady.orR` (\"any exe ready\") which is too permissive — it fires when only exe0 is ready but the slot is blocked from exe0 by age-based arbitration (e.g., qAge(0)=0 means slot 0 is younger than slot 1, and opsReady(1)=1 means slot 1 is also ready, blocking issue0_0 via `(qAge(0) | ~opsReady(1))`). Fix: Replaced `io.exeReady.orR` in all three liveness triggers with the OR of each slot's specific issue-enable conditions (exe0 path viable OR exe1 path viable), accounting for age checks and issue0 forwarding. This ensures the trigger only fires when slot i genuinely has a viable path to an execution unit. All three assertion labels preserved unchanged. DUT logic untouched. `valid_onehot0` remains as a separate issue."
  ],
